ulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)\\to 6$ and $g(x)\\to 2$ with $g$ nonzero near $x_0$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}(f/g)(x)$?","options":["$8$","$3$","$12$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff7a6216bce334fa4577e171"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_laws_for_functions_c83e5d3f_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can the quotient law fail to be available when $M = 0$?","options":["Because the quotient law needs a nonzero denominator","Because $f$ must then be undefined","Because products of functions cannot converge.","Because max and min laws require $M = 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e006d007df51bd80c24e4492"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_laws_for_functions_c83e5d3f_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which operation is explicitly included in the limit laws for functions besides sums, products and quotients?","options":["Taking arbitrary derivatives","Taking logarithms of negative values","Taking local extrema of $f$","Taking $\\max\\{f,g\\}$ and $\\min\\{f,g\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bfdd6c841a1cc93e48706940"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_laws_for_functions_c83e5d3f_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)\\to-1$ and $g(x)\\to 4$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\min\\{f,g\\}(x)$?","options":["$4$","$3$","$-1$","$-4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"604ea6f08aca63d483530bc6"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_laws_for_functions_c83e5d3f_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A student applies the quotient law with $g(x)\\to 0$ and concludes $\\lim(f/g) = L/0$. What is the best correction?","options":["The quotient law always gives $0$","The quotient law always gives $+\\infty$","The quotient law should be replaced by the product law without checking anything","The quotient law in the theorem cannot be used because the limiting denominator is zero"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"96f65ae4a38c2362e1fa1d6c"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f(x)\\to L$ and $g(x)\\to M$, what does the product law give?","options":["$(fg)(x)\\to LM$","$(fg)(x)\\to L+M$","$(fg)(x)\\to L/M$","$(fg)(x)\\to \\max\\{L,M\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b9d5ad8f1da369c68833c43"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which operation is covered by the max law for function limits?","options":["$\\max\\{f,g\\}$","$f\\circ g$ only","$f^{-1}$ only","$f'$ only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bd0346cf26948e5e12dc42cb"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must be true before applying the basic algebraic limit laws to $f$ and $g$?","options":["Both functions must be polynomials.","Both relevant limits exist as real numbers.","Both functions must be strictly increasing.","Both functions must be undefined at $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"39e4e2de10d6c49f1ff871ef"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)\\to -1$ and $g(x)\\to 4$, what is $\\lim (f-g)(x)$?","options":["$3$","$-4$","$-5$","$5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7fc710840b5306b0c0c35b9d"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)\\to -2$ and $g(x)\\to 5$, what is $\\lim\\min\\{f,g\\}(x)$?","options":["$5$","$3$","$-2$","$-10$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cd681fd900f24b9740720e49"},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_laws_for_functions_add_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","q